# Water Channel MD Simulation — My Personal Notes

setup: 10 water molecules Temperature is kept at 300 K (room temperature).

---
## What Each File Does

| File | Created by | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `generate_water.py` | Me (Python) | Places 10 water molecules randomly in the box, writes the .data file |
| `water_channel.data` | generate_water.py | Starting structure |
| `water_channel.in` | Me (text editor) | Instruction script for LAMMPS |
| `log.lammps` | LAMMPS | Log of everything printed to terminal during the run. |
| `traj.lammpstrj` | LAMMPS | Trajectory file: atom positions saved every 100 steps. Open in OVITO. |

---
## The Simulation Box

```
Box dimensions:
  X = 20 Angstroms   (flow direction — periodic)
  Y = 10 Angstroms   (channel height — walls at top and bottom)
  Z = 10 Angstroms   (depth — periodic)
```

One Angstrom (Å) = 0.1 nanometres = 0.0000000001 metres.
A water molecule is about 2.8 Å across.
So this box fits roughly 3–4 molecules in each direction. It is **extremely tiny**.

---

## Boundary Conditions — What Happens at the Edges

This is one of the most important concepts in MD.

| Direction | Type | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| X (left/right) | Periodic (`p`) | Molecule leaving right reappears on left. Mimics an infinitely long channel. |
| Y top | Reflecting wall (`wall/reflect yhi`) | Hard wall. Molecules bounce off elastically. |
| Y bottom | Reflecting wall (`wall/reflect ylo`) | Mirror/symmetry plane. Also acts as a hard wall. |
| Z (front/back) | Periodic (`p`) | Same as X. Wraps around. Mimics infinite depth. |

The LAMMPS line that sets this is:
```
boundary   p f p
```
- `p` = periodic
- `f` = fixed (non-periodic, we handle it manually with walls)

The walls are set with:
```
fix  WALL_TOP  all wall/reflect yhi EDGE
fix  WALL_BOT  all wall/reflect ylo EDGE
```
`yhi` = top face of box, `ylo` = bottom face. `EDGE` means exactly at the box boundary.

---

## The Water Model — SPC/E

**SPC/E** (Simple Point Charge — Extended). It is the most widely used
water model in engineering MD and is good for bulk water properties at room temperature.

### SPC/E molecule geometry (rigid, never changes shape)


- O–H bond length: **1.0 Å**
- H–O–H angle: **109.47 degrees**
- The molecule is **completely rigid** — it never bends or stretches during simulation

| Atom | Type | Charge |
|---|---|---|
| Oxygen | 1 | −0.8476 e |
| Hydrogen | 1 | +0.4238 e each |

---

## The Force Field — How Atoms Interact

The force field is the set of equations describing how atoms push and pull each other.
I am using `lj/cut/coul/cut` which has two components:

### 1. Lennard-Jones (LJ) potential — van der Waals forces

Handles the physical size of atoms and weak attraction between them.
- At very short range: **strong repulsion** (atoms cannot overlap)
- At medium range: **weak attraction** (van der Waals)
- Beyond 10 Å: **ignored** (cutoff)

In SPC/E, only **Oxygen–Oxygen** pairs have LJ interactions.
Hydrogen has no LJ parameters (it is essentially a point charge in this model).

```
pair_coeff   1 1   0.15535  3.16600    # O-O: epsilon=0.15535 kcal/mol, sigma=3.166 Å
pair_coeff   2 2   0.00000  0.00000    # H-H: no LJ
pair_coeff   1 2   0.00000  0.00000    # O-H: no LJ
```

- `epsilon` = depth of the energy well (how strong the attraction is)
- `sigma` = distance at which the LJ potential is zero (roughly the atom diameter)

### 2. Coulomb (electrostatic) potential — charge interactions

Handles the attraction between opposite partial charges (O and H from different molecules)
and repulsion between same charges (H...H). This is what creates hydrogen bonds.

I am using a **simple cutoff Coulomb** (`coul/cut`) with a 10 Å cutoff.
This means: beyond 10 Å, electrostatic interactions are ignored.

> Note: cutoff Coulomb is not the most accurate method for bulk water, but it works
> for this basic simulation. For better accuracy, PPPM or Ewald summation is used
> (we will upgrade to that later).

---

## SHAKE — Keeping Water Rigid

SPC/E water is supposed to be rigid, but LAMMPS by default lets all atoms move freely.
The **SHAKE algorithm** enforces the rigid geometry by constraining bond lengths and angles
every timestep.

```
fix  SHAKE  all shake 0.0001 100 0 b 1 a 1
```

- `b 1` = constrain all bonds of type 1 (O–H bonds)
- `a 1` = constrain all angles of type 1 (H–O–H angle)
- `0.0001` = convergence tolerance
- `100` = maximum iterations per timestep

Why use SHAKE? Because without it, the fast O–H bond vibration (~3500 cm⁻¹) would
require a very small timestep (~0.5 fs). SHAKE removes this vibration and lets us
use a larger timestep of **2 fs**, making the simulation 4× faster.

---

## The Thermostat — NVT Ensemble

I am running in the **NVT ensemble**:
- **N** = Number of atoms is constant (no atoms created or destroyed)
- **V** = Volume is constant (box size does not change)
- **T** = Temperature is controlled (kept near 300 K)

```
fix  NVT  all nvt temp 300.0 300.0 100.0
```

- First `300.0` = starting target temperature (K)
- Second `300.0` = ending target temperature (K)
- `100.0` = thermostat damping time (femtoseconds)

The thermostat used is **Nosé–Hoover**. It works by coupling the system to a
"heat bath" — if the system gets too hot, kinetic energy is removed; if too cold,
energy is added. The 100 fs damping time controls how quickly temperature is corrected.
Too small = unphysical oscillations. Too large = poor temperature control.

---



## The data file — water_channel.data

This file tells LAMMPS the **initial state** of the system.

```
30 atoms          <- 10 molecules × 3 atoms each
20 bonds          <- 10 molecules × 2 O-H bonds each
10 angles         <- 10 molecules × 1 H-O-H angle each

2 atom types      <- type 1 = Oxygen, type 2 = Hydrogen
1 bond types      <- type 1 = O-H bond
1 angle types     <- type 1 = H-O-H angle

0.0 20.0 xlo xhi  <- box goes from x=0 to x=20 Å
0.0 10.0 ylo yhi  <- box goes from y=0 to y=10 Å
0.0 10.0 zlo zhi  <- box goes from z=0 to z=10 Å
```

Each atom line:
```
atom_id  mol_id  atom_type  charge   x        y        z
1        1       1          -0.8476  7.992    7.704    6.391   <- Oxygen of molecule 1
2        1       2           0.4238  7.176    7.126    6.391   <- H1 of molecule 1
3        1       2           0.4238  8.809    7.126    6.391   <- H2 of molecule 1
```

---

## The Input Script — water_channel.in (line by line)

```lammps
units           real
```
Use "real" units: distance in Å, time in femtoseconds, energy in kcal/mol, temp in Kelvin.

```lammps
atom_style      full
```
"full" style stores: atom ID, molecule ID, atom type, charge, x, y, z.
Required for charged molecular systems like water.

```lammps
boundary        p f p
```
X = periodic, Y = fixed (we add walls manually), Z = periodic.

```lammps
pair_style      lj/cut/coul/cut 10.0
```
LJ + cutoff Coulomb, both with 10 Å cutoff.

```lammps
read_data       water_channel.data
```
Load the starting structure.

```lammps
minimize        1.0e-4 1.0e-6 500 5000
```
Before running dynamics, minimise energy first. This moves overlapping molecules apart.
Without this, the initial forces are so large that molecules explode out of the box
(we learned this the hard way — the "Lost atoms" error).

```lammps
timestep        2.0
run             1000
```
Each step = 2 femtoseconds. Run for 1000 steps = 2 picoseconds total simulation time.

---

## Errors and What They Meant

| Error | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Cannot use non-periodic boundaries with PPPM` | PPPM requires all 3 directions to be periodic, but Y was set to `f` | Switched to cutoff Coulomb (`coul/cut`) which has no periodicity requirement |
| `Cannot use fix wall/reflect in periodic dimension` | `wall/reflect` does not work in a periodic direction | Set Y to `f` (fixed) in the boundary command |
| `Missing argument for fix wall/lj93` | `EDGE` keyword only works with `wall/reflect`, not `wall/lj93` | Used `wall/reflect` with `boundary p f p` instead |
| `Lost atoms: original 30 current 24` | Initial atom positions had molecules too close together — forces were explosive | Added energy minimisation step before the MD run |

